locking: rwsem: spin longer before yielding the CPU

rwsem_spin_on_owner() calls cpu_relax() on every turn of its wait.
Skip it for the first thousand turns, so a lock held for a very short
time is taken without going round the scheduler.

The cost is real and has to be weighed, not assumed: on x86 cpu_relax()
is the PAUSE instruction, which is what lets a hyperthread sibling use
the core and lets the CPU drop its frequency. A thousand turns without
it is a thousand turns of a hot loop, on a laptop on battery as much as
on a desktop. To measure with hackbench and tests/bench/frame.c, and
with power draw on a laptop, not only with throughput.

From XanMod's 7.0 series.

--- a/kernel/locking/rwsem.c
+++ b/kernel/locking/rwsem.c
@@ -746,6 +746,7 @@
 	struct task_struct *new, *owner;
 	unsigned long flags, new_flags;
 	enum owner_state state;
+	int i = 0;
 
 	lockdep_assert_preemption_disabled();
 
@@ -782,7 +783,8 @@
 			break;
 		}
 
-		cpu_relax();
+		if (i++ > 1000)
+			cpu_relax();
 	}
 
 	return state;
